  "title": "Every node type occurring in a real corpus is classified; every consulted literal exists in the pinned grammar; every unconsulted name-carrier's loss is measured",
  "instrument": {
    "file": "script/m12/bash-node-types-compare.ts",
    "replaces": "script/m12/bash-node-types-legacy-inventory.ts",
    "reason": "the retired instrument hardcoded its 35-entry 'referenced' list instead of measuring it, and never computed observed\\referenced, so it could not fail and hid 21 unclassified corpus node types",
    "authorities": {
      "grammar": "Language.load(wasm/tree-sitter-bash.wasm) nodeTypeForId — the same oracle F1 uses; no node-type list is written in this file",
      "source": "analyzer string literals at node-type positions, extracted by parsing src/ast/real-bash-analyzer.ts with the tree-sitter TypeScript grammar (the F1 method, not a regex)",
      "corpus": "pinned git revisions read with `git show HEAD:<path>`, so the bytes measured are the committed bytes",
      "loss": "production parseFile edge endpoints compared against the names inside each unconsulted name-carrier"
    },
    "noHardcodedNodeTypeList": true,
    "selfAudit": "This instrument carried three defects of its own before it was trusted, all of the species it exists to catch: (1) it hardcoded its referenced list; (2) it attributed a name-bearing node to its own type instead of to its nearest non-carrier ancestor, which made every container look empty and the gap count structurally zero; (3) it iterated OBSERVED types to find dead branches, so a literal the grammar cannot produce - which by definition never appears in a tree - was invisible, and it reported unknown_type 0 while F1 was reporting bash's `identifier` dead branch. Each was found by a number that contradicted an independent measurement, not by inspection. The reasons are recorded here and in the commit so the next reader does not re-add them."
  },
